rz/sz -- Chuck Forsberg's XMODEM/YMODEM/ZMODEM file transfer programs.

This directory is rz 1.31 / sz 1.44, dated 1988 (rz.c line 1, sz.c line 1).

The files name their authors but carry no permission statement of their own,
which is what upstream shipped: the grant for rz/sz was published in the
ZMODEM protocol document rather than in the source.  This file records the
terms that were found and says plainly where each one comes from.


1.  What is stated inside these files
-------------------------------------

Two grants, both in rbsb.c, both reproduced verbatim.

rbsb.c lines 350-360 -- Stephen Satchell and Chuck Forsberg, on the CRC
update macro:

    /*
     * updcrc macro derived from article Copyright (C) 1986 Stephen Satchell.
     *  NOTE: First srgument must be in range 0 to 255.
     *        Second argument is referenced twice.
     *
     * Programmers may incorporate any or all code into their programs,
     * giving proper credit within the source. Publication of the
     * source routines is permitted so long as proper credit is given
     * to Stephen Satchell, Satchell Evaluations and Chuck Forsberg,
     * Omen Technology.
     */

rbsb.c lines 364-367 -- Gary S. Brown, on the 32-bit CRC table:

    /*
     * Copyright (C) 1986 Gary S. Brown.  You may use this program, or
     * code or tables extracted from it, as desired without restriction.
     */

Authorship, with no terms attached, is stated at rz.c line 7 (`rz.c By Chuck
Forsberg') and zm.c lines 1-4 (`ZMODEM protocol primitives / 11-10-87 Chuck
Forsberg Omen Technology Inc').  rz.c line 44 notes that the directory-creating
routines come from "Public Domain TAR by John Gilmore".


2.  Forsberg's own grant, which is NOT in this tree
---------------------------------------------------

Forsberg placed rz/sz in the public domain, but he did so in the ZMODEM
protocol specification, not in the source files.  That document is not held by
this project, so the following is quoted from it at second hand and is flagged
as such rather than presented as something found here.  The ZMODEM protocol
document, sections 3.4 and 22, states:

    ZMODEM was developed for the public domain under a Telenet contract.  The
    ZMODEM protocol descriptions and the Unix rz/sz program source code are
    public domain.  No licensing, trademark, or copyright restrictions apply
    to the use of the protocol, the Unix rz/sz source code and the ZMODEM
    name.

Anyone relying on that sentence should obtain the protocol document and read
it, rather than relying on this transcription.  The upstream distribution this
directory came from contains no licence file either, so there is nothing
further to vendor from the holding.


3.  What this file does not do
------------------------------

It grants nothing, and it does not assert that section 2 reaches these
particular files.  It records what is here and what is not.
